\section{Introduction}

Temporal Knowledge Graphs (TKGs) extend traditional static graphs by associating time stamps with facts, enabling the representation of dynamic world knowledge. This added temporal dimension allows TKGs to model richer events and relationships, making them highly valuable for applications such as link prediction and time-aware recommendation systems. However, the inherent noisiness of real-world data presents a significant challenge. Given the vast search space of possible time-stamped facts, it is difficult to determine which statements are most probable at a specific time. Consequently, there is a growing need to capture uncertainty and learn probability distributions over time-stamped facts, especially when data is sparse.

Traditional TKGC methods generally fall into two categories: Transductive Learning and Inductive Learning. Transductive learning treats time-aware knowledge graph completion as extending transductive link completion to the temporal domain. Inductive learning, by contrast, employs generative modeling techniques to capture uncertainty alongside time-aware representation learning. Although some methods have attempted to introduce time-aware variational autoencoders for joint learning, they lack uncertainty modeling for time-stamped facts, a feature essential for handling sparse data. Additionally, many existing approaches fail to account for two critical aspects of time-aware graph completion: Joint Learning and Uncertainty Modeling.

